// cleave.map — Straf3 course 2, "Cleave"
//
// Valve 220 source. This file is the SINGLE source of the course geometry:
// straf3-map compiles it to both the collision hulls and the render mesh, so
// the thing you see and the thing you hit cannot drift apart. Edit it as a
// .map (TrenchBroom, Radiant) — do not mirror these numbers into code.
//
// No BSP and no PVS, so the world does not need to be sealed and there are no
// ceilings; brushes exist only where the player can reach them.
//
// coil is a line. Cleave is a CHOICE: one course, two ways through the middle
// of it, and the runner picks on the day from the speed they arrive with.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MEASURED vs PREDICTED. Every number below carries one of those two words and
// they are not interchangeable. "Measured:" means a published artefact shows
// the real mover producing it — probes/course-lab/results/cleave.txt, generated
// by `probes/course-lab`, which drives the SHIPPED compiler's collider.
// "Predicted:" means arithmetic from the continuous model, which is how a lip
// gets placed but is not evidence; the discrete sub-stepped integrator moves
// such numbers by a few per cent, and a few per cent moves a gate.
//
// NOT DONE, stated plainly rather than left to be discovered:
//   - Never played by a human, by operator decision for this whole milestone.
//     No claim here rests on feel and none should be read as one.
//   - The two branches' completion TIMES are not yet measured, so the crossover
//     speed at which the leap starts beating the sweep does not yet exist in
//     any form, predicted or otherwise. Section S3 says what is known instead.
//   - The maximum speed a runner can deliver to the launch lip is UNMEASURED,
//     and it is the number this map most depends on. See THE OPEN QUESTION.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The line, and why each piece is the shape it is. Physics: gravity 800,
// jump 270, step 18, min_walk_normal 0.7, hull (-15,-15,-24)..(15,15,32).
// A jump lasts 0.675 s flat and peaks 45.6 units up. "ups" is horizontal speed
// in units/second. Tick rate 125 Hz.
//
//   S0  y -832..32     Start room, 896 x 864, spawn in the west at angle 0.
//                      Deliberately coil's start room, to the unit: 768 units
//                      of eastward run and a 90-degree left arc, a circle jump
//                      executed inside the room, crossing the start line at the
//                      mouth already fast. Copied rather than reinvented so
//                      that entry speed at the line is comparable between the
//                      two courses, which is worth more than novelty here.
//
//   S1  y 32..1472     Run-up corridor, 448 wide, 1440 long. Pure acceleration,
//                      and the only section whose job is to let the runner
//                      decide how fast they will arrive at the split.
//                      checkpoint 0 sits at y 1376..1440: the last common
//                      ground, and the near side of the fork.
//
//   S2  y 1472..1920   THE APRON, and the split. The corridor mouth opens into
//                      a chamber 2016 wide. Straight ahead and to the east is
//                      flat floor at z=0 — the low line, which costs nothing to
//                      enter. To the WEST the floor rises: ramp_climb runs from
//                      z=0 at x=-448 up to z=128 at x=-768 (normal.z 0.9285,
//                      comfortably walkable), onto pad_climb_top.
//                      THE POINT OF ENTERING SIDEWAYS: the high line's first
//                      four hundred units go across the course rather than
//                      along it. A runner — or a search — that scores the next
//                      half second by ground gained toward the finish reads
//                      that as a loss and takes the low line. The cost is real
//                      and it is paid before the leap is even in sight.
//                      Measured: the ramp's own surface. A survey along
//                      (0,1400) -> (-700,1900) -> (-700,4700) every 32 units
//                      finds 115 samples, 0 void, normal.z 0.9285..1.0000.
//
//   S3  y 1920..4608   THE FORK. Two lines, and the geometry that keeps them
//                      apart is brushwork rather than bookkeeping: wall_divide_s
//                      and wall_divide_n stand at x -480..-448 from z=-32 to
//                      z=512. That is 384 units above the highest surface
//                      adjacent to them (the mesa at z=128) against a flat-jump
//                      apex of 45.6, so no line crosses the divider. Below and
//                      beside it the mesa's own east face is 160 units tall.
//
//     THE HIGH LINE — the leap.  x -896..-480, at z=128.
//                      pad_high_lane gives 1024 units of elevated run-up from
//                      the turn onto the lip at y=2944. Then 448 units of open
//                      air over floor_slot, onto pad_high_land at the same
//                      height.
//                      Measured, swept over speed x approach yaw across a
//                      +/-15 degree band from (-672, 2932, 152):
//                        600 ups clears the slot head-on;
//                        620 ups clears it from EVERY approach in the band;
//                        below 600 every approach lands in the slot.
//                      Measured, and this is the gate's teeth: arriving at
//                      560..590 ups and falling short, you leave the slot floor
//                      with 145..153 ups. You keep about a quarter of what you
//                      brought.
//
//     THE LOW LINE — the sweep.  x -448..1088, at z=0.
//                      No gate, no threshold, forgiving of any entry speed, and
//                      longer: block_c1 (x -448..704, y 2432..2752) forces the
//                      line east of x=704, and block_c2 (x 192..1088,
//                      y 3520..3840) then forces it back west of x=192. Two
//                      long carved turns instead of one committed jump — and a
//                      sustained turn is where a strafejumper GAINS speed, so
//                      the sweep is longer in distance and may well be faster
//                      in arrival speed. Which of those wins is the map's
//                      question, and see NOT DONE: it is not yet answered.
//                      ramp_low_out (y 4096..4608, normal.z 0.9701) climbs back
//                      to z=128 to rejoin.
//
//     ONE-WAY PERMEABILITY, on the record. The divider is deliberately ABSENT
//                      across the slot's y-range 2944..3392. It has to be:
//                      there is no kill plane anywhere in straf3-sim, so a
//                      runner who fell short into a sealed slot would be inside
//                      a box with 160-unit faces on three sides and a run that
//                      could never end. The gap makes the slot drain east into
//                      the low line. So low -> high is impossible everywhere,
//                      and high -> low is possible in exactly one place, at the
//                      cost of the leap and three quarters of your speed.
//                      Whether that failure path is genuinely slower than
//                      simply taking the low line is a TIME comparison that has
//                      not been made. Until it has, this is one-way
//                      permeability with the penalty measured at the speed
//                      level only.
//
//   S4  y 4608..4992   The rejoin, full width at z=128. checkpoint 1 sits at
//                      y 4608..4672: the far side of the fork, and the first
//                      common ground since checkpoint 0.
//
//   S5  y 4992..5664   The finish. 352 units of open air from the lip onto a
//                      ledge 16 units higher and 384 wide.
//                      Measured, same two-dimensional sweep, from
//                      (0, 4980, 152): 510 ups head-on, 530 ups from every
//                      approach in the band. Below 510 you land on
//                      ramp_finish_bail (normal.z 0.809, walkable both ways),
//                      which runs you down to the pit floor and lets you walk
//                      back up to the approach pad and try again — the ledge's
//                      front face is 272 units tall and cannot be climbed.
//                      No upper bound was found: at 1000 ups the landing is
//                      y=5348 against a ledge spanning 5344..5664, and above
//                      that the finish volume is crossed in flight anyway,
//                      which is why pit_floor reaches y=6720.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE OPEN QUESTION, which is this map's biggest risk and is recorded rather
// than answered.
//
// The leap's gate is only a gate if the lip is hard to arrive at fast. There is
// no absolute speed clamp in the simulation — `max_speed` 320 is the g_speed
// wish-speed base, not a ceiling — and CPM air control accumulates speed on a
// sustained turn. An automated search left circling in the apron of an earlier
// cut of this map reached 1437 ups against coil's measured peak of 783.
// If a runner can deliver much more than 620 ups to the lip, then the leap is
// free, the sweep is strictly dominated, and this stops being a fork at all.
// The number that decides it is the MAXIMUM speed deliverable at the lip, and
// it is not measured yet. It is not honest to publish a crossover speed before
// that exists, so none is published.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHICH LINE A RUN TOOK, without a checkpoint on either branch.
//
// r35 requires the fork to lie between two consecutive spine checkpoints with
// neither branch carrying one, so the route cannot be read from the checkpoint
// bitmask. It does not need to be. Two disjoint boxes settle it from position
// alone, which any replay's --trace supplies, so the test is mechanical and is
// derived from the shipped binary rather than self-reported by a probe:
//
//   HIGH  the hull is inside x -896..-448, y 1920..4608, z   96..640
//   LOW   the hull is inside x -448..1088, y 1920..4096, z  -32..96
//
// Every completing run enters exactly one of them.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHECKPOINT SEMANTICS: SPLITS. Declared on worldspawn as
// "checkpoint_mode" "splits", meaning a run that crosses START and FINISH
// without visiting a checkpoint is a complete and valid run.
//
// NOTHING IN THE TREE READS THAT KEY TODAY. Not straf3-map, not straf3-sim,
// not straf3-game, not straf3-records, not straf3-replay. It is a declaration
// awaiting a reader, and the engine does not enforce it. The same is true of
// the "count" keys on the target_checkpoint entities below.
//
// Splits rather than gates because this map is a fork, and gating a checkpoint
// on one branch would make that branch mandatory — a map about choosing between
// two lines cannot require both.
//
// Checkpoint numbering comes from SOURCE ORDER and only from source order; the
// compiler assigns it while walking the entity list. The "count" values here
// are written equal to their source-order index (0, then 1) so the two readings
// agree by construction. Note this differs from coil.map, whose counts are
// 1-based for the same indices; agreeing with the compiler is the property that
// matters, and unifying the two files is a separate job.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Surface families are named for what the simulation does with them, not for
// what they look like. Cleave uses four: straf3/floor and straf3/pad are flat,
// straf3/ramp is walkable everywhere it appears here (normal.z 0.809 to 0.9701)
// and straf3/wall is what a hull cannot pass. It deliberately uses NO
// straf3/slide and no straf3/ramp45: coil owns the surfaces that are barely
// walkable or not walkable at all, and cleave's difficulty is route choice
// rather than footing. Nothing on this course requires a crouch, and no gate
// on it admits a standing hull and a crouched one differently.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// entity 0 — worldspawn
{
"classname" "worldspawn"
"message" "Cleave"
"author" "Straf3"
"checkpoint_mode" "splits"
{
